## Verdict

Passed. `studio/briefs/W-110.md` revision 4 takes the single SHOULD from the
round-6 prereview (`studio/ci/W-110-spec-6.log`, 0 MUST) and the optional
residual note. Two one-line hardenings; eight behaviours and every finding
count unchanged.

## What changed

**7(k)'s path clause is now `/\/[\w.-]+\//`.** I re-measured both clauses on
the exact message rather than taking the finding on trust, and the censor is
right on both halves: the real string is **157 characters** — inside the 160
cap — and `/(?:^|\s)\//` answers **false** on it, because Node quotes the path
(`lstat '/tmp/claude-1000/…'`) so the leading slash follows an apostrophe, not
whitespace. Revision 3's clause therefore missed the one leak it was written to
catch. `/\/[\w.-]+\//` answers true on it and on macOS's
`/var/folders/ab/cd12/T/…`, while all seven of the helper's fixed refusal
strings pass (`path must remain under ci/` and both `escapes ci/` strings have
no second slash) and so does every message this opus can emit — the `is
missing` wording, the bare-code wording, the manifest-header wordings. I kept
the clause rather than dropping it and leaning on assertion 2: the temp-root
substring check only catches the *test's own* root, so it would not notice a
path from anywhere else, and the regex is the assertion that actually states
the property.

**The `code === undefined` branch gains a shape guard.** Taken, and taken as a
guard rather than as the enumerated-membership check offered. The refusal is
passed through only if it matches `^[\x20-\x7e]{1,80}$` and does not match
`/\/[\w.-]+\//`; otherwise the message carries the bare word `refused`. An
allowlist of the seven strings would pin a set that is fixed today but lives in
another function, so its closedness would be *inferred*; validating the shape
cannot drift, and a future refusal that embedded a path could not leak through
it. That is the same move as the `redact` cap — structural rather than
incidental — which is why it was worth the line. Step 4 defers to step 1's
switch and says so.

## Not changed

No behaviour added or removed (`countBehaviours` is 8). No finding count moved:
the guard only shortens a message, and the regex only tightens an assertion
about messages, so behaviour 7's stated counting convention and every "exactly
one"/"two" under behaviours 6 and 7 stand as signed at round 5. The two
surviving mentions of `(?:^|\s)\/` are deliberate citations of the superseded
clause, in 7(k)'s explanation and the revisions row.

